[Thinking: I'm working through a dynamic programming approach where I compute the number of paths from each node to the destination B, then use prefix sums to answer range queries efficiently in O(1) time per query after O(N) preprocessing.]

This is a straightforward **DAG path-counting DP**, made efficient by processing nodes from `B` backward and using a **suffix prefix-sum array** to answer each node's range sum in O(1) instead of O(n_i).

**Recurrence:** Let `ways[i]` = number of distinct paths from `i` to `B`.
- `ways[B] = 1`
- For `i < B`: `ways[i] = ways[i+1] + ways[i+2] + ... + ways[min(i+n_i, B)]`

Since `gateway i` can only reach forward nodes, computing `ways[]` from `B` down to `1` guarantees all needed values are ready. A suffix-sum array `pre[]` (where `pre[i] = ways[i] + pre[i+1]`) turns each range sum into O(1).

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

const long long MOD = 1e9 + 7;

int main(){
    int N, B;
    scanf("%d %d", &N, &B);

    vector<int> n(N + 1);
    for(int i = 1; i <= N; i++) scanf("%d", &n[i]);

    // ways[i]: number of paths from i to B
    // pre[i] = ways[i] + ways[i+1] + ... + ways[B]  (suffix sum, 1-indexed up to B)
    vector<long long> ways(B + 2, 0), pre(B + 2, 0);

    ways[B] = 1;
    pre[B] = 1;
    pre[B + 1] = 0; // sentinel

    for(int i = B - 1; i >= 1; i--){
        int hi = min(i + n[i], B);
        if(hi >= i + 1){
            // sum of ways[i+1 .. hi] = pre[i+1] - pre[hi+1]
            ways[i] = ((pre[i + 1] - pre[hi + 1]) % MOD + MOD) % MOD;
        } else {
            ways[i] = 0;
        }
        pre[i] = (ways[i] + pre[i + 1]) % MOD;
    }

    int Q;
    scanf("%d", &Q);
    while(Q--){
        int A;
        scanf("%d", &A);
        printf("%lld\n", ways[A]);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

**Correctness notes:**
- Nodes with index `> B` are irrelevant to any path ending at `B`, so we never even need their `n_i` values (they're still read from input but simply not used in the DP array, which only spans `1..B`).
- The suffix-sum trick avoids the naive O(n_i) sum per node, collapsing total DP construction to O(B).

**Complexity:** O(N + B) preprocessing (reading + DP), O(1) per query → O(N + Q) overall, which should comfortably handle large N, B, Q (up to 10⁵–10⁶ range typical for these problems).

**Edge case:** If `n[i] == 0` for some intermediate gateway, `hi < i+1`, so `ways[i] = 0` correctly (that gateway is a dead end, contributing nothing).

